/** @page ncurses_toolsguide Tools and REPL Helpers

    @tableofcontents

    @section ncursestoolsqrepl Full-Screen Qore REPL

    The installed \c qrepl command provides an interactive %Qore console with menus, a file
    browser, captured output, history, and a scrollable output pane. Start it in a terminal:
    @verbatim
qrepl --help
qrepl
    @endverbatim

    Enter evaluates the draft; Alt-Enter inserts a hard newline. Up/Down navigate
    history for a single-line draft and move visually within multiline input.
    Ctrl-P/N always navigate history; Ctrl-R opens a literal history search.
    The unfinished draft and selection return after moving past the newest entry.
    Errors retain the draft. Ctrl-Z/Y undo/redo; Shift-arrows select text.
    Ctrl-C/X/V use an application-local clipboard shared with transcript selection.
    This does not modify the desktop clipboard. Ctrl-A/E move to the hard-line
    boundaries; Ctrl-K/U delete toward a hard-line boundary; Ctrl-W deletes a word.

    PageUp/PageDown scroll output independently of the draft. Scrolling up pauses
    following; the status bar reports unread lines. Ctrl-End or View > Latest Output
    resumes following. F6 switches focus between draft and output; focused output
    supports arrows/Home/End, Shift-click selection and Ctrl-C copy. Ctrl-S or
    View > Search Output opens literal search. F3/Shift-F3 visit next/previous matches.
    Bracketed paste inserts one undoable operation without evaluating embedded newlines.
    Output retains the latest 10,000 logical lines; the editor grows to six rows and
    then scrolls within its allocation. Tab completion uses the visible wrapped caret.

    Use <tt>/help</tt> for console commands. Ctrl-Q or Escape opens exit confirmation.
    Alt-key menu shortcuts provide file, edit, view and help actions. Command history
    is persisted in <tt>~/.qore-repl-history</tt>. Output capture preserves complete
    lines and marks unterminated output with a reversed percent sign. Capture workers
    enqueue text only; widget mutation and drawing stay on the UI thread.

    @section ncursestoolshelpers Building a Custom Console

    @ref ncursesrepluiintro "NcursesReplUi" provides editing primitives independently of the
    full-screen program. @ref NcursesReplUi::InputBuffer "InputBuffer" tracks text and cursor
    position, while @ref QoreHistory::History "History" stores commands. The module reexports
    the history API. This example edits a command and saves it without opening a terminal:

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires NcursesReplUi

NcursesReplUi::InputBuffer buffer();
buffer.insertChar("1 + 2");
buffer.moveHome();
buffer.insertChar("print ");
@assert(buffer.getText() == "print 1 + 2");
QoreHistory::History history();
history.add(buffer.getText());
@assert(history.prev() == "print 1 + 2");
hash<auto> layout = NcursesReplUi::LayoutCalc::calcLayout(24, 80);
@assert(layout.output_rows == 21);
    @endcode

    @ref NcursesReplUi::LayoutCalc::calcLayout() "LayoutCalc::calcLayout()" divides terminal
    dimensions into output, input, and information panes;
    @ref NcursesReplUi::ScrollCalc "ScrollCalc" computes bottom-anchored scrolling positions.
    Pair these helpers with @ref NcursesUi::ScrollableWidget "ScrollableWidget" and
    @ref NcursesUi::StatusBarWidget "StatusBarWidget" as described in
    @ref ncurses_ui_widgetsguide "UI Widget Toolkit".
*/
